---
title: Existing projects and file changes
description: Discover native projects, run their tools, and review changes to Rivet-owned source lists.
section: Guides
order: 16
updated: '2026-10-13'
---

Open a file inside a CMake, Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, or configured Lua project and run `:RivetTask`. Rivet recognizes `CMakeLists.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `Package.swift`, and Lua configuration markers `.luarc.json`, `.luacheckrc`, or `.stylua.toml`. For CMake it searches past nested source-list fragments for a project declaration or presets; an independently declared nested project can be selected as its own root. `--root PATH` explicitly chooses a CLI root. Discovery writes no manifest and does not claim ownership of existing source lists.

## Work in an existing CMake project

1. Run `:RivetTask configure`. Select an available native configure preset. Without preset files, Rivet offers Debug and Release under `build/rivet/debug` and `build/rivet/release`, using Ninja.
2. Run `:RivetBuildTarget` to select a target discovered through CMake's File API.
3. Run `:RivetTask build` or `:RivetTask test`. Native build/test presets remain authoritative when present.
4. Run `:RivetRunTarget` to select a configured executable. Run resolves its real artifact, including a custom output name or directory.

Running an external CMake application before its first configure starts configuration to discover targets. `rivet run --root .` configures, discovers, builds, and runs when there is one executable. With several, choose a target; an unknown target is rejected after discovery. Explicit CLI target names also work:

```sh
rivet configure --root .
rivet targets --root .
rivet run --root . --target viewer
```

CMake scripts execute during configuration as they normally do. Rivet writes its File API query in the selected build directory and a compilation-database copy in `.cache/rivet`. It preserves custom `.clangd` files; point `CompilationDatabase` at `.cache/rivet` to follow the selected build. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/).

A multi-config project needs a build preset with a configuration. Build directories and executable artifacts must be inside the project; symlink paths and in-source builds are rejected. Create native presets before using the cache override editor on a project without presets.

## Create files without adopting a build

`:RivetFile` can create source files in an existing project or an ordinary folder. Select the language and review the paths; the operation preserves the native build configuration and creates no Rivet manifest. Register or import those files through the project's native workflow. In an ordinary folder, the CLI needs an explicit root:

```sh
rivet file scripts/prepare.lua --language lua --kind script --root . --preview
```

External Lua tasks discover `.lua` files beneath the selected root for syntax checks. Run is available for `main.lua`, and Test expects `tests/test.lua`. These external projects default to the `lua` runtime; an executable override in `.rivet.local.json` must be compatible with ordinary Lua invocation. Use a Rivet-owned Lua project when you need the recorded Neovim runtime and plugin test workflow. See [Lua projects](/docs/lua/).

## Review a move or removal

For files registered in `.rivet.json`, `:RivetFiles` offers a project-relative move/rename or removal. The review shows deleted files, new contents, the manifest change, and regenerated CMake source lists. `q` cancels; `c` applies.

```sh
rivet move src/core/extra.cpp src/core/detail.cpp --preview
rivet move src/core/extra.cpp src/core/detail.cpp
rivet remove src/core/detail.cpp --preview
```

File moves preserve bytes and module identity. Update affected `#include` paths and C++ identifiers explicitly; this is not a C++ semantic refactoring engine. Keep the extension, and avoid leaving a target without a compiled source. Removing an imported or implemented module is refused until its registered references have been addressed.

The apply step checks the preview snapshots again and refuses changed files, symlinks, destination collisions, and unsaved Neovim buffers. Failed writes roll back committed file contents. After applying in Neovim, open buffers follow moved paths, removed-file buffers close, and clean buffers for rewritten files refresh. Saving a moved buffer writes to its new path. Files are removed only after the review is applied.

## Rename a module's identity

Use `:RivetModuleRename` separately from a filename move. It previews module declarations, implementation declarations, and imports in registered project files, plus the manifest mapping.

```sh
rivet module-rename simulation.core simulation.work --preview
```

The conservative token scan skips comments and string literals. It handles direct named declarations/imports; it is not a compiler-based refactor across dependencies, macro-generated code, partitions, or unregistered files. Inspect the diff, apply, then build and test.

## Ownership stays explicit

Registered file-list mutations require a Rivet manifest. They do not adopt arbitrary existing CMake source lists. C/C++ source operations and recorded foreign-language moves/removals preserve that ownership boundary. Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM tasks preserve native manifests and lockfiles; use their native tools for dependency changes. See the [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/), and [Lua](/docs/lua/) guides.

Automatic source-list adoption is a separate future migration workflow. Existing-project build support does not imply that migration has happened.
